WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.690 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it's Sunday morning I think my room is  dark my head is throbbing I haven't not  in my stomach nausea mix with anxiety oh  why did I take all those tequila shots  last night why do I feel like I need to  apologize to my best friend again I  promised myself just like I did last  Sunday the going forward things will be  different no more shots I am done  feeling like this when I wake up week  after week I made these Sunday morning  promises to myself only to break them by  Friday night I just couldn't let go of  alcohol it played a huge role in my life  both socially and professionally I was  afraid that if I quit drinking I would  feel different from everyone else or  that I wouldn't be seen as part of the  group yet I knew I had to make a change  after years of struggling to keep my  Sunday morning promises I finally sought  support to quit drinking last summer and  trust me that was a change for the  better I'm sure many of you may relate  to my story it's not that dramatic just  a bunch of ugly Sunday mornings but even  so I found quitting drinking really hard  in the early days I couldn't go to bars  or be around people drinking alcohol and  I'd say I still had an easier time than  a lot of people for those with more  ingrained addiction it can be nearly  impossible to quit drinking and if you  are able to quit you face a world that  too often revolves around alcohol  getting sober is helped me understand  that keeping alcohol in such a prevalent  role in society disadvantages and  excludes those who don't drink  whether people abstain due to pregnancy  religious beliefs recovery or personal  preference we are creating a divide that  keeps many non-drinkers out of our most  important conversations this exclusion  whether intentional or not  means we're missing out on the valuable  untapped perspectives of many of these  people  and we are not talking about a small  group of people here in fact alcohol org  a subsidiary of American addiction  centers estimates that 30 percent of US  adults don't consume any alcohol and  this number continues to rise as  Millennials become more and more  health-conscious there's even a new  trend taking hold called the sober  curious movement with the non-drinking  portion of the population on the rise  now is the time for us to figure out how  to include everyone in our business and  social interactions the first thing we  can do is change our events many  networking events today take place at  bars given my history a bar with  unlimited alcohol is not an appealing  place for me to meet people for instance  earlier this year I was at a GSB  networking event I was walking around in  circles without a drink desperately  trying to find the courage to join a  group to chat with and let me tell you  going up two groups of strangers at a  400 person event with no social  lubricant is really hard every time I  try to join in with someone new they  would try to give me a drink or ask  about why I wasn't drinking and for the  people I was talking to well at the end  of the night a lot of them weren't  making any sense  for me the experience was miserable I  dreaded going to future events but knew  I had to or risk missing out on the  connections and relationships that stem  from many of these interactions and in  these situations I'm one of the lucky  ones  I can still tolerate going to these  events even if I'd prefer not to my  voice is still heard but many  non-drinkers aren't so lucky and will  miss out on key opportunities by staying  away from alcohol focused events in the  name of self-care we can start to level  the playing field for non-drinkers by  thinking about alternative alcohol-free  ways for us to meet and interact with  one another  planning a hike or a cooking class  there's a team event in lieu of a happy  hour is all it takes to start increasing  inclusivity the next thing we can do is  think about the alternatives we offer it  should be just as easy to find a soda as  it is to find a gin and tonic  keeping non-drinkers limited to just  water or whatever mixers are on hand  well everyone else gets to choose from a  plethora of alcoholic options sends the  signal that non-drinkers are an  afterthought or worse weren't thought of  it all and I don't know about you but  when I feel like an afterthought time  and time again I stop showing up if we  don't show non-drinkers that they're  welcome they'll stop coming to our  events luckily coming up with  alternatives is a really easy way to  have a big impact earlier this year  all of us MBA ones participated in the  executive challenge it was a full-day  competition that culminated in a squad  celebration and a class-wide cocktail  hour as I walked to the room where my  squad would be having our post  competition powwow  I felt dread take over me as I heard  pops of champagne coming from every  single room around me great I thought  another time or I'll be the only person  not drinking I'd be grudgingly walked to  my team room only to see my squad fellow  sitting there with a huge smile and a  bottle of sparkling grape juice my  immediately changed I didn't feel like  an afterthought or an outsider what was  a small gesture from him completely  changed the way I saw my role in the  group changing our events and our  alternatives are just two ways that we  can start to have an impact but we can  have an even bigger impact by changing  our dialogue around alcohol about a  month after I got sober I met some  friends for brunch in San Francisco we  sat down at our table and a waiter came  over to walk us through the menu when he  got to the drinks section he joked if  you're here to have a good time here are  the cocktails if you're no fun here are  the mocktails a month earlier I would  have thought the joke was cute but in  that moment it almost reduced me to  tears  I was upset about having to quit  drinking I was feeling really different  from my friends you could all drink  normally I felt alone due to my  inability to participate in a favorite  group pastime I didn't even know who I  was as a sober person in just one  sentence that waiter managed to put my  worst fears into words now that I wasn't  drinking  I figured I'd be no fun later that same  day a friend at dinner started to make a  toast  I raised my glass of water hoping to go  unnoticed however I was quickly  chastised Cheers thing with water is bad  luck  I thought a lot about why I drank about  why it was so hard for me to keep my  Sunday morning promises for a while  alcohol was fun it helped me feel  confident when inside it was  self-conscious but most importantly it  helped me feel like part of the group  but all those illusions started to fall  away as I lost control over my  relationship with alcohol for me  drinking is no longer fun we need to  change the narrative around what is  normal and fun when it comes to alcohol  use making someone feel abnormal or like  they're no fun because they're not  drinking it's not okay  a good rule of thumb keep your comments  about drinking and not drinking to  yourself and if you hear someone making  a comment that might make a non drink  you're uncomfortable I encourage you to  be the one to step in and make it right  we here at the GSB have a special  responsibility to make a change as  future leaders Society will look to each  of us as role models we can start to  make a change today by changing our  events or alternatives and our dialogue  around alcohol so with that I'd like to  raise a toast with water because it is  not bad luck to each of us here today  each of us has the power to leave this  room and make a difference both here at  the GSB and beyond so cheers to changing  the future to creating environments  where both drinkers and non-drinkers can  thrive thank you  [Applause]  [Music]  you

